**THERE IT IS!** Line 237-240 in wlroots:

```c
if (seat->keyboard_state.focused_surface == surface) {
    // this surface already got an enter notify
    return;
}
```

wlroots itself has the same SKIP check! Even though we removed the SKIP from our code, wlroots silently drops the re-delivery. The `wlr_seat_keyboard_enter()` function returns immediately if the surface is already focused.

This means our `same=1` re-deliveries via the timer are being silently dropped by wlroots. The Wayland `wl_keyboard.enter` event is never sent to the client/XWayland.


Now I see the pattern - Super+K works because it goes through `focusclient()` which bails when the surface hasn't changed, but when switching away and back, the surface actually changes so both `focusclient()` and wlroots properly send the leave+enter sequence. The solution is to clear the wlroots seat focus before re-delivering by calling the clear focus function, then we can re-enter the same surface.

This matches what KWin does in their fix - they clear focus to NULL first, then set it to the target window, forcing both X server and wlroots to send a fresh FocusOut+FocusIn sequence. So I need to modify the seat keyboard focus function to detect when it's the same surface and still perform the clear-then-set dance to trigger the proper event sequence.

Implementing this now...

